{"state": "KESTREL VALLEY COMMUNITY COLLEGE \u2014 HARDSHIP BURSARY REGULATIONS 2026/27\n(Approved by the Student Finance Committee, 2026-05-12. Applies to awards for the academic year starting 2026-09-01.)\n\nPART A \u2014 AWARD LEVELS\nA.1 The bursary is paid at one of five levels:\n    Level 0 \u2014 no award\n    Level 1 \u2014 EUR 500\n    Level 2 \u2014 EUR 1,200\n    Level 3 \u2014 EUR 2,000\n    Level 4 \u2014 EUR 3,000\nA.2 The level is determined by the PER-CAPITA COUNTABLE INCOME of the assessed household (Part D), using the table in Part E, subject to the asset rule in Part F.\n\nPART B \u2014 DEPENDENCY STATUS\nB.1 A student is INDEPENDENT if on the reference date (1 September of the award year) at least one of the following is true:\n    (a) the student is 24 years old or older;\n    (b) the student is married;\n    (c) the student has at least one dependent child living with them;\n    (d) the student was in local-authority care after their 16th birthday.\nB.2 Otherwise the student is DEPENDENT.\nB.3 For all purposes of these regulations, a registered civil partnership is treated exactly like a marriage, and a registered civil partner exactly like a spouse.\nB.4 Dependency status is fixed on the reference date; later changes apply only from the next award year.\n\nPART C \u2014 ASSESSED HOUSEHOLD\nC.1 Dependent students: the assessed household is the parent(s) the student normally lives with, their spouse/partner if any, the student, and all other children living in that household.\nC.2 Independent students: the assessed household is the student, their spouse (if any), and their dependent children (if any). Parents' income and household are NOT assessed for independent students, even if parental data was supplied on the form.\nC.3 Household size = number of persons in the assessed household.\n\nPART D \u2014 COUNTABLE INCOME\nD.1 Countable income = sum over the assessed household (excluding children under 18) of: gross employment income for the previous calendar year (2025), net self-employment profit, pensions, and taxable benefits.\nD.2 Excluded from countable income: child benefit; disability-related allowances; student loans and grants; earnings from the college's WORK-STUDY programme (for any student); one-off gifts under EUR 1,000.\nD.3 Dependent students only: deduct EUR 2,000 from the household's countable income for each OTHER child of the household enrolled full-time in higher education.\nD.4 Per-capita countable income = countable income \u00f7 household size, rounded to the nearest euro.\n\nPART E \u2014 LEVEL TABLE (per-capita countable income, EUR)\n| Per-capita countable income | Level |\n|-----------------------------|-------|\n| up to 7,000                 | 4     |\n| 7,001 \u2013 10,500              | 3     |\n| 10,501 \u2013 14,000             | 2     |\n| 14,001 \u2013 18,000             | 1     |\n| above 18,000                | 0     |\n\nPART F \u2014 ASSETS\nF.1 If the assessed household's savings and investments (excluding the main home and one car) exceed EUR 40,000, the level is capped at Level 1.\nF.2 Assets are declared on the form; the college may request statements.\n\nPART G \u2014 PROCEDURE (reference)\nG.1 Applications close 2026-10-15. Late applications are assessed only if funds remain.\nG.2 Evidence: P60/annual payslip or tax assessment for each income line; certificate for civil status; enrolment certificates for siblings.\nG.3 Payments are made in two instalments (November, February) to the student's own account.\nG.4 Students must be enrolled at least half-time on the census date (2026-10-01).\nG.5 Appeals go to the Student Finance Committee within 28 days of the decision letter.\nG.6 Assessors record the dependency decision, the household composition, each countable and excluded income line, and the per-capita figure on the assessment sheet, so that the decision can be checked by a second assessor.\nG.7 Amounts are taken in whole euros as declared and evidenced; the college does not index 2025 income to 2026 prices.\n\nPART H \u2014 FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS\nQ: I filled in my parents' details because the form asked for them. Does that matter? \u2014 A: The form collects parental data from everyone. Whether it is used depends only on Part B.\nQ: My partner and I are not married but have lived together for years. \u2014 A: Cohabitation without marriage or registered civil partnership does not make a student independent.\nQ: My work-study job is my main income. \u2014 A: Work-study earnings are still excluded (D.2).\nQ: My sibling is at university; can I deduct that? \u2014 A: Only dependent students' households get the D.3 deduction.\n\nPART I \u2014 WORKED EXAMPLES (illustrative, fictional students)\nI.1 Dependent student, age 19, lives with mother (employment 2025: EUR 31,000) and brother (17). Household 3. Countable 31,000. Per capita 10,333 \u2192 Level 3.\nI.2 Dependent student, age 21, lives with both parents (employment EUR 48,000 and EUR 12,000) and a sister (22) studying full-time at university. Household 4. Countable 60,000 \u2212 2,000 (D.3) = 58,000. Per capita 14,500 \u2192 Level 1.\nI.3 Independent student, age 26, single, no children. Employment EUR 11,400; work-study EUR 2,900 (excluded). Household 1. Per capita 11,400 \u2192 Level 2.\nI.4 Independent student, age 22, married, one child aged 2. Spouse employment EUR 24,600; student no income; child benefit EUR 3,000 (excluded). Household 3. Per capita 8,200 \u2192 Level 3.\nI.5 Independent student, age 30, savings EUR 52,000, income EUR 6,000. Per capita 6,000 \u2192 Level 4 by table, capped to Level 1 by F.1.\n\nPART J \u2014 RELATED FUNDS (not assessed on this form)\nJ.1 Childcare Support Fund: separate application; up to EUR 1,500 per year towards registered childcare.\nJ.2 Travel Grant: students living more than 40 km from campus may claim 50 % of a public-transport season ticket.\nJ.3 Emergency Loan: interest-free, up to EUR 800, repayable within the academic year.\nJ.4 Receiving a hardship bursary does not affect eligibility for J.1\u2013J.3.\n\nPART K \u2014 DATA PROTECTION\nK.1 Information supplied is used only to assess student-finance applications and is deleted 6 years after the last payment.\nK.2 Parental information supplied for independent students is deleted at the end of the assessment and is not used.\nK.3 Applicants may request a copy of their data at any time from the Student Finance Office.\n\nPART L \u2014 CHANGE LOG\n2026-05-12  Part E thresholds raised by approx. 5 % for inflation. D.2 extended: work-study earnings now excluded for ALL students (previously dependent students only).\n2025-05-20  B.3 added (registered civil partnerships).\n2024-06-01  F.1 asset cap raised from EUR 30,000 to EUR 40,000.\n\n=====================================================================\nAPPLICATION HB-26-0417 \u2014 received 2026-09-10\n=====================================================================\nSection 1 \u2014 Student\n  Name: Mara Voigt       Student no. KV-2291807      Programme: AS Laboratory Technology, full-time\n  Date of birth: 2002-10-15\n  Civil status: registered civil partnership (certificate dated 2026-06-20, partner: Lea Brandt)\n  Dependent children: none\n  Local-authority care history: none\n  Address: M\u00fchlweg 3, 34131 Kassel (shared with partner)\n\nSection 2 \u2014 Student's and partner's income, calendar year 2025\n  | Person      | Source                                        | Amount EUR |\n  |-------------|-----------------------------------------------|-----------:|\n  | Mara Voigt  | Part-time job, Apotheke am Markt (employment) |      7,200 |\n  | Mara Voigt  | KVCC work-study (library assistant)           |      3,600 |\n  | Mara Voigt  | Birthday gift from grandmother                |        500 |\n  | Lea Brandt  | Employment, Stadtwerke Kassel (gross)         |     20,600 |\n  Evidence attached and checked by the office: annual payslips for both employments, KVCC work-study statement, civil-partnership certificate.\n\nSection 3 \u2014 Parents' household (completed by applicant)\n  Father: Jens Voigt, employment income 2025: EUR 64,000\n  Mother: Sabine Voigt, employment income 2025: EUR 22,000; child benefit received: EUR 3,000\n  Other children in parents' household: Tim Voigt (20, full-time university student), Lena Voigt (15)\n  Parents' address: Am Hang 12, 34270 Schauenburg\n\nSection 4 \u2014 Assets\n  Mara and Lea joint savings account: EUR 9,400. No investments. Car: one (2014 hatchback).\n\nAssessor's first note (trainee): \"Mara is 23 on 1 Sept \u2192 dependent. Use parents: 64,000 + 22,000 \u2212 2,000 (Tim) = 84,000 over 5 persons = 16,800 \u2192 Level 1.\"\n", "model": "ruvector-native-submission-round2-v2", "questions": {"decision": {"type": "score", "instructions": "Apply the bursary regulations to application HB-26-0417. What award level (0\u20134) should Mara receive?", "criteria": ["Level 0 \u2014 no award", "Level 1 \u2014 EUR 500", "Level 2 \u2014 EUR 1,200", "Level 3 \u2014 EUR 2,000", "Level 4 \u2014 EUR 3,000"]}}}